+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+A tool executor is written against its declared `inputSchema`, but nothing between the model's tool call and the executor ever read that schema: `ToolRegistry.execute` looked the tool up and handed the input straight through, so a malformed call was answered by whichever cast-and-default the executor happened to carry. This spec pins the contract that closes that gap: the registry validates what the model sent against the same schema the providers were given — not a second copy in another format — and turns a schema-rejected call into a tool result the model can read and correct inside the existing tool-round budget.
+
+## The execute contract
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` validates a call's input against the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs, and a call the schema rejects returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` describes the failure instead of throwing ([validated by: returns a rejection to the model and never runs the executor when a required property is missing, while an unknown tool name still throws](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L71)).
+- An unregistered tool name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>`, so the split is deliberate: schema-rejected input is a tool result, an unknown name is a caller error ([validated by: returns a rejection to the model and never runs the executor when a required property is missing, while an unknown tool name still throws](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L71)).
+- A wrong-typed field is rejected with a message naming the field path and the expected type, and neither the returned text nor a log line carries the offending value, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed field naming the path and expected type, and emits the offending value neither in the result nor the log](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L100)).
+
+## Registration and resolution
+
+- A static tool's schema is compiled at `register` time, so a schema no validator can compile throws at registration and names the tool rather than failing mid-conversation ([validated by: throws at registration for a schema no validator can compile, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L128)).
+- A schema produced by a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved for every call, and a call is validated against the schema current at that call rather than one cached from an earlier round ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call, enum included](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L149)).
+
+## What validation enforces
+
+- Validation enforces `required`, declared types and `enum` and nothing else — no coercion, no defaults, no injected `additionalProperties`, and no opt-out flag — so input the schema accepts reaches the executor exactly as the model sent it, extra properties included, exactly once ([validated by: feeds a rejected call back to the model so a corrected retry succeeds, passing accepted input through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L180)).
+- A declared `enum` is enforced, so a value outside the allowed set is rejected the way a missing or mistyped property is ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call, enum included](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L149)).
+- A rejected call reaches the model as the tool's result, so the same conversation can correct the call and retry within the tool-round budget instead of ending the stream in a transport error ([validated by: feeds a rejected call back to the model so a corrected retry succeeds, passing accepted input through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L180)).
+
+## Compiled once per static schema
+
+- A static tool's schema is compiled once and reused across calls rather than recompiled on every `execute`.
